PUBLIC RESPONSE: J.  Impossible to tell from the given statement

The sign pattern of a causal path is a property of the causal mechanism C → … → Y.  It cannot be identified from a manipulation that creates no variation in the putative cause.

C is nectar caffeine concentration.  Citrus × sinensis, the orange, does not constitutively synthesise caffeine in nectar, or in any other tissue, in the wild.  The background level of C in an orange is therefore effectively zero under ordinary conditions.  “Knocking out caffeine production” in a plant that never makes caffeine in the first place leaves the experimental C distribution coincident with the control C distribution, Cexp ≡ Cctrl ≡ 0.  No contrast in C is generated, so there is no contrast in F , R or Y that can be attributed to C and the a,b,c,d,e path coefficients are not identifiable in this system.

In the biological system where the model was originally derived, e.g. Coffea spp., the literature signs that are normally discussed are

* c > 0  caffeine increases pollinator learning / fidelity / retention;
* d > 0  greater retention increases per-plant pollination and total yield;
* a < 0  the stimulant effect of caffeine shortens the mean time a bee spends on a single flower;
* b < 0  a longer mean time per flower, at fixed overall foraging time, reduces the number of flowers visited and hence the system level pollination throughput;
* e < 0  caffeine biosynthesis is a metabolic cost to the plant, so higher C is associated with a lower net yield for a given pollination input.

That is the a : – , b : – , c : + , d : + , e : – pattern, i.e. option B, that is normally quoted for the coffee-pollinator system.  In the orange knockout as stated the manipulation does not produce the required variation in C, so the signs are in principle not testable here.

